Following the first actual QR call one memory operation at a time
================================================================

This note begins at the machine-address zero entry of the recorded
dqrls.o x86-64 object, NOT at an abstract QR formula. The raw
instructions and encodings are in ../dqrls.x86-64.disasm.txt and
the complete first-pass machine crosswalk is in
dqrls.x86-64.instructions.english.

This is an instruction-driven SYMBOLIC reconstruction checked by
reading the printed machine instructions. It is not an observed
register/memory trace, does not demonstrate physical x86 execution,
and does not describe the dynamically linked BLAS library.

Declaring the starting memory and calling boundary
--------------------------------------------------
Let Original stack pointer denote the eight-byte address in RSP as
the called routine begins. The address [Original stack pointer] holds
the saved caller return address. This value is not assigned a fake
literal number.

Under the System V AMD64 calling convention used by this historical
Debian x86-64 GFortran/C build, the first six pointer arguments arrive
in these 64-bit registers:
  RDI contains a pointer to the first double of matrix x.
  RSI contains a pointer to the four-byte integer n.
  RDX contains a pointer to the four-byte integer p.
  RCX contains a pointer to the first double of array y.
  R8 contains a pointer to the four-byte integer ny.
  R9 contains a pointer to the double tol.

The seven remaining pointer arguments are in eight-byte stack slots,
relative to Original stack pointer:
  +0x08: pointer to b
  +0x10: pointer to rsd
  +0x18: pointer to qty
  +0x20: pointer to k
  +0x28: pointer to jpvt
  +0x30: pointer to qraux
  +0x38: pointer to work

Each of these Fortran arguments is passed BY ADDRESS in this case;
loading a pointer is not the same operation as loading the value
referred to by that pointer. These statements describe the observed
ordinary ABI/caller contract, not all possible calling conventions.

Beginning at execution-index offset 0x0
--------------------------------------
The instruction beginning at 0x0 is the two-byte encoding 41 57.
Its original spelling is "push r15".
Read the old value of R15, decrease RSP by eight, and store the old
R15 at the newly addressed stack slot. Provided no fault occurs,
the execution index becomes 0x2. This is one instruction, not a
higher-level "save registers" operation.

At 0x2, bytes 31 c0, "xor eax,eax" store zero in EAX and thus clear
the high 32 bits of RAX. They set the logical-result flags;
this is NOT identical to flag-preserving assignment of zero.

At 0x4, bytes 49 89 cf, "mov r15,rcx" copy the Y-array pointer to R15.

At 0x7, bytes 48 89 d1, "mov rcx,rdx" copy the P-integer pointer
to RCX, freeing RDX to take another argument later.

At offsets 0xa, 0xc, 0x11, 0x16 and 0x17, push R14, R13, R12,
RBP and RBX in that exact order. Together with the first push,
the six pushes allocate 48 bytes and preserve six old register
values. The stack pointer now equals Original stack pointer
minus 48 bytes; the order of saved values is not interchangeable.

At 0xe, copy the NY-integer pointer from R8 into R13.
At 0x13, copy the N-integer pointer from RSI into R12.
These copies happen between the stack pushes and do not affect
the already saved old values of R13 or R12.

At 0x18, subtract hexadecimal 0x60 (96 decimal) from RSP.
The stack pointer now equals Original stack pointer minus
144 bytes, denoted Current frame pointer for this note.
No arithmetic has yet been done on the contents of matrix x.

At 0x1c, read the four bytes addressed by RSI, interpret them
as a signed 32-bit integer, sign-extend to 64 bits and store
in RBP. This is the dereferencing of *n*, NOT another pointer copy.

At 0x1f, read the four bytes addressed by RDX, interpret them
as a signed 32-bit integer, sign-extend to 64 bits and store
in RSI. This is the dereferencing of *p*.

At 0x22, save the pointer to p at memory address
Current frame pointer + 0x38. That pointer is distinct from
the integer value of p already copied to RSI.

At 0x27, read an eight-byte pointer from memory address
Current frame pointer + 0xa0. Algebraically this address is
Original stack pointer + 0x10, and the loaded value is the
rsd-array pointer; place it in RBX. A change in RSP changes the
meaning of a fixed [rsp+...] notation, which is why simply
transcribing operand names is not a sufficient explanation.

At 0x2f, copy R12 to RDX so that RDX now holds the pointer to n.
At 0x32, test signed n stored in RBP against itself to establish
condition flags. At 0x35, save the NY pointer to
Current frame pointer + 0x30. At 0x3a, copy the tolerance
pointer from R9 into R8.

At 0x3d, "cmovs rbp,rax" conditionally copies zero from RAX
into RBP when the sign flag from the n test is set.
This is the machine's chosen computation of a nonnegative
loop bound from n. Even when no copy occurs, it is not a branch.

At 0x41, test signed p in RSI against itself. At 0x44,
save the original x-array pointer to the local stack address
Current frame pointer + 0x08. At 0x49, "cmovns rax,rsi"
copies p to RAX only if p was nonnegative, otherwise RAX
remains zero. Again: conditional MOVE, not a branch.

At 0x4d, restore the N-integer POINTER from R12 into RSI.
At 0x50, write the nonnegative p bound from RAX into
Current frame pointer + 0x40. At 0x55, copy that bound to R14.

Preparing all nine arguments for the next machine call
------------------------------------------------------
Before the next pushes:
  RDI is the x pointer.
  RSI is the n pointer.
  RDX is the n pointer, intentionally repeated.
  RCX is the p pointer.
  R8 is the tolerance pointer.
  The sixth argument register R9 is not yet populated for the call.

At 0x58, evaluate [rsp+0xc8] BEFORE changing RSP.
RSP still equals Original stack pointer minus 144.
Consequently, the effective address equals Original stack
pointer plus 56 bytes: it holds the pointer to work.
Push this work pointer. RSP decreases by eight bytes.

At 0x5f, evaluate [rsp+0xc0] using the NEW RSP.
The effective address is Original stack pointer plus 40:
read and push the pointer to jpvt.

At 0x66, evaluate [rsp+0xd0] using RSP after the SECOND push.
The effective address is Original stack pointer plus 48:
read and push the pointer to qraux.

The three new stack slots, as observed by the CALLED
procedure, now hold qraux, jpvt and work in argument order.
Reading all three source expressions against the same old RSP
would have produced the wrong answer.

At 0x6d, read [rsp+0xc8] with RSP now 168 bytes below the
original. The effective address is Original stack pointer
plus 32, which holds the pointer to k; copy that pointer to R9.

At 0x75, bytes e8 00 00 00 00 form a relative CALL whose
encoded displacement has not been resolved in the object.
The associated relocation is
  76: R_X86_64_PLT32 dqrdc2_-0x4
so the printed apparent destination "0x7a" is merely an
unlinked placeholder. The linked call targets dqrdc2_.
The CALL saves 0x7a as its return byte address on the stack
and transfers execution to the linker-resolved routine,
not to the 0x7a continuation itself.

The actual call argument values are therefore:
  Argument 1: x pointer, through RDI
  Argument 2: n pointer, through RSI
  Argument 3: n pointer again, through RDX
  Argument 4: p pointer, through RCX
  Argument 5: tolerance pointer, through R8
  Argument 6: k pointer, through R9
  Argument 7: qraux pointer, at first stack argument address
  Argument 8: jpvt pointer, at second stack argument address
  Argument 9: work pointer, at third stack argument address

Only AFTER deriving this from the machine operations can
we cross-check the exact call in ../dqrls.f:
  call dqrdc2(x,n,n,p,tol,k,qraux,jpvt,work)
This agreement is a source/assembly correspondence, not
a substitute for inspecting the encoded machine operations.

Returning to dqrls after the unknown machine work of the call
-------------------------------------------------------------
If the called dqrdc2_ eventually returns normally, execution
resumes at 0x7a. Without actually following the callee, its
effects on x, k, qraux, jpvt and work remain unresolved here.
The instruction at 0x7a reads [rsp+0xc8], now naming the
pointer to k, into RAX.

At 0x82, add 32 bytes to RSP: this discards the three
eight-byte stack arguments plus eight bytes of frame padding.
RSP is now Original stack pointer minus 136.

At 0x86, read the four-byte signed or unsigned bit pattern
addressed by RAX, the k pointer; store it in EAX, which zeroes
RAX's upper 32 bits. At 0x88, test EAX against itself.
At 0x8a, branch to 0x1cd when the signed interpretation
of that rank is less than or equal to zero (ZF=1 or SF != OF).
Otherwise continue at 0x90.

At 0x90, read the four-byte ny value through R13 into EDX.
At 0x94, establish its zero/sign flags. At 0x96, branch
to 0x155 if that signed integer is less than or equal to zero.
Only otherwise does execution continue into the later
solution loop. The loop's longer machine path is preserved
instruction-by-instruction in the full ledger; it is not
claimed to be semantically validated by this shorter note.

What this demonstrates and what remains
---------------------------------------
The machine code explicitly constructs a by-reference Fortran
call by moving pointer values among register and stack slots.
Some short mnemonics hide real dependencies on changes to RSP;
some "conditional" instructions do not transfer control;
a nominal immediate call destination can be a relocation
placeholder. These facts can be recovered bottom-up.

This note does NOT yet:
  - prove the exact behavior inside dqrdc2_ or dqrsl_;
  - explain the full floating-point and branch state paths;
  - supply dynamic BLAS instruction bodies or memory maps;
  - derive the observed least-squares coefficients by executing
    these symbolic steps;
  - validate equivalence to the Fortran source for all inputs;
  - translate the routine into a distinct ARM architecture.

Those are separate acceptance requirements. Actual R lm()
uses squared VERTICAL residuals, not the proposed unsquared
perpendicular-distance fit.
